.he "ccc (1)                      8/11/26                      ccc (1)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
ccc
- C compiler


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
ccc [options] file ...


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Ccc
is the supervisor for the C compiler.  It sorts its arguments by
suffix and runs the passes that each one needs.

Arguments ending in ".c" are C source and are put through the whole
chain:
.bd
pass0
lexes and preprocesses,
.bd
c0
parses,
.bd
c1
generates code,
.bd
asz
assembles.
Arguments ending in ".s" are assembler source and are assembled.
Arguments ending in ".o" or ".a" are objects and archives and are
passed to
.bd
ld
to be linked.  Unless told otherwise the result is left in "a.out".

Nothing about where the compiler lives is compiled in.
.bd
Ccc
works out where its passes are from the path it was invoked by: the
directory it was found in, with "lib" beside rather than under it, so
/bin/ccc uses /lib and /usr/local/bin/ccc uses /usr/local/lib.  A name
typed with no slash in it says nothing about where the shell found it,
and only that case falls back to the directory the driver was built
for.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-o file	Write the output to
.bd
file.
With -c or -s it names the object or the assembly instead, and only
one input file may be given.  The default is "a.out".

.ti -10
-c	Compile and assemble, but do not link.  Leaves one ".o" per
source.

.ti -10
-s	Compile only, leaving the assembly in a ".s" file.  The
assembler is not run.

.ti -10
-O	Run
.bd
peep
over the assembly before it is assembled.  This is worth a few percent
of the text, which on this machine is the difference between a program
fitting and not.

.ti -10
-k	Keep every intermediate - the ".x", ".tok", ".nam", ".ast",
".dat", ".s" and ".o" - rather than removing them on the way out.  For
looking at what a pass did.

.ti -10
-m sys	Produce binaries for
.bd
system,
which is "micronix" or "cpm".  This selects the startup file and the
system-call library only; every target's libraries sit side by side in
the one directory.

.ti -10
-I dir	Add
.bd
dir
to the list searched for include files in quotes.

.ti -10
-i dir	Set the directory searched for include files in angle
brackets.  The default is worked out from where the driver was found,
and is /usr/include on an ordinary installation.

.ti -10
-Dname	Define
.bd
name
to the preprocessor, as though by a #define.  With no value it is
defined as 1.

.ti -10
-l x	Link with the library "libx.a", searched for in the library
directories.

.ti -10
-L dir	Add
.bd
dir
to the list searched for libraries.

.ti -10
-S	Strip the symbol table from the output.

.ti -10
-9	Write nine-character symbols in the output rather than
fifteen, for objects that have to be read by older tools.

.ti -10
-E	Preprocess only, and write the result to standard output.

.ti -10
-H	Give
.bd
c0
the readable ".tok" file rather than the ".x" lexeme stream.
Slower, and for reading rather than for building.

.ti -10
-x	Print each command as it is run.

.ti -10
-n	Print the commands without running any of them.

.ti -10
-C flags	Pass -v
.bd
flags
to
.bd
pass0.

.ti -10
-1 flags	Pass -v
.bd
flags
to
.bd
c0.

.ti -10
-2 flags	Pass -v
.bd
flags
to
.bd
c1.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/lib/pass0 - the preprocessor and lexer.

/lib/c0 - the parser.

/lib/c1 - the code generator.

/lib/peep - the peephole optimizer, run for -O.

/lib/asz - the assembler.

/lib/ld - the linker.

/lib/libc.a - the C library.

/lib/libu.a - the Micronix system-call library; libcpm.a is CP/M's.

/lib/crt0.o - the startup file; crtcpm.o is CP/M's.

/tmp/ccc* - the intermediates, removed on the way out unless -k.
.sp 2

.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
pass0 (1), c0 (1), c1 (1), peep (1), asz (1), ld (1), wslib (1),
